<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Genocide Convention</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1948</span> · Also known as Convention on the Prevention and Punishment of the Crime of Genocide</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">Genocide</a> Convention, formally the Convention on the Prevention and Punishment of the Crime of Genocide, was adopted by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations-general-assembly">United Nations General Assembly</a> on 9 December 1948 and came into force in 1951. Drawing on the work of the lawyer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raphael">Raphael</a> Lemkin and the experience of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holocaust">Holocaust</a>, it defines genocide as acts intended to destroy a national, ethnic, racial or religious group and obliges states to prevent and punish it.</p></div>
